<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Columbanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">543–<a href="/years/615">615</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Columbanus, Columban</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Columbanus was an Irish monk from Bangor who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> around 590 and founded monasteries at Annegray, Luxeuil and Fontaine in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His strict monastic rule and Irish customs, such as the dating of Easter, brought him into conflict with Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-ii">Theuderic II</a> and Queen Brunhild expelled him in 610. He went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he founded the abbey of Bobbio and died in 615.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columbanus.jpg" alt="Columbanus" width="320" height="630" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Colombano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Columbanus, the Latinised form of Colmán, meaning little dove, was born in the Kingdom of Leinster in Ireland. He was first educated under Abbot Sinell of Cluaninis, whose monastery stood on an island of the River Erne in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-fermanagh">County Fermanagh</a>, and under Sinell he composed a commentary on the Psalms. He then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey">Bangor Abbey</a> to study as a teacher of the Bible, learning grammar, rhetoric and geometry, and Abbot Comgall taught him Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. He stayed at Bangor until about 590, when Comgall reluctantly allowed him to travel to the continent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Columbanus sailed with twelve companions, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a>, and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> in Brittany. In Burgundy King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> welcomed them and granted land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges Mountains</a>, where they turned a ruined Roman fortress into a school. It drew so many students that they moved to Luxeuil and founded a second school at Fontaines, all under his authority and following the Celtic tradition in which he had been educated. In 603 he and his followers quarrelled with the Frankish bishops over the date of Easter, since the Irish used their own calculation, and the bishops also objected to the Irish tonsure.</p><p>In 611 he reached the court of Theudebert II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and travelled up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to preach to the Suebi and Alemanni, settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a> preached and a monastery was founded. When war in 612 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-ii">Theuderic II</a> control of Austrasia and some of his students were murdered, he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> he was welcomed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and wrote against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>. In 614 Agilulf gave him land at Bobbio. At the king's request he also wrote to Pope Boniface IV about the controversy over the Three Chapters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last year King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a> invited him to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, but he declined and asked the king to protect his monks at Luxeuil. He retired to a cave above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a> river and died at Bobbio on 21 November 615. His Rule embodied the customs of Bangor and other Irish monasteries and was much shorter than the Rule of Saint Benedict, with ten chapters beginning with absolute obedience. His teaching on penance stressed private confession to a priest, followed by penances imposed in reparation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a> stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> until his death, and the Abbey of Saint Gall was founded at the site of his cell about seventy years later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Columbanus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The church grew immensely rich through royal and aristocratic gifts, and monasteries multiplied, especially after the Irish monk <b>Columbanus</b> founded Luxeuil around 590; the abbey of Saint-Denis near Paris, where Dagobert I was buried in 639, became closely tied to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish monk <b>Columbanus</b> founded Luxeuil there, but was expelled by King Theuderic II and Brunhild.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Leinster, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Bobbio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=589" aria-label="Leinster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4411 329 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Columbanus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=589">What was here in 589 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
